2026-10-21 19:03:10 techniques: {"techniques":["1. Recursive QAOA (RQAOA): Run the shallow chain circuit, read off the most strongly correlated pair (or most polarized cavity), and fix or eliminate it. The network then predicts phases for the smaller residual chain, so each round's projected cost loses less information.","2. Warm-start QAOA (Egger et al.): Bias the initial state and the mixer toward a classical relaxation, such as an SDP or belief-propagation solution. On each cavity, the network outputs a tilted product state plus a matching \"warm\" mixer instead of a uniform superposition.","3. Grover Mixer QAOA (GM-QAOA, Bärtschi & Eidenbenz): Use a mixer that reflects about the state-preparation unitary. Equal-cost states then get equal amplitude, and the network only has to learn the per-layer angles. The local version applies this per cavity, so near-degenerate levels aren't arbitrarily favoured.","4. Quantum Alternating Operator Ansatz with constraint-preserving XY mixers (Hadfield et al.): Restrict each cavity's mixer to its feasible levels (a ring or complete-graph XY mixer). Infeasible levels then never get amplitude and don't have to be penalized through the lossy cost phase.","5. Parameter transfer / concentration with INTERP/FOURIER initialization (Zhou et al.): Have the network predict a few low-frequency Fourier coefficients (u, v) that generate the gamma/beta schedule across layers, instead of every angle directly. Use INTERP to extrapolate from depth p to p+1, so schedules stay smooth and 
